        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          OBJECTIVES:<BR>
The radiation environment seems to be the most important constraint for long-term manned missions. Also, over time, problems arise from the effects of densely ionizing radiation on electronics, computers and other material. The current knowledge about biological radiation effects and radiation protection cannot be applied to the space environment without a number of difficulties in physical and biological aspects, one of which is the unknown composition of the radiation environment in space.
<p>
Through the interaction with spacecraft materials, the space radiation produces recoil nuclei, nuclear reaction products and secondary radiation, like thermal radiation caused by the slowing-down effect of the radiation particles. The observed effects depend on altitude, geographic or geomagnetic latitude, solar activity and the amount, as well as the type and placement of shielding material in the spacecraft. Thus, the radiation level can vary in orders of magnitude. In the Apollo spacecraft for instance, the shielding varied from about 1.75 to 216 g/cm<sub>2</sub>, depending on the direction of the incoming radiation. Therefore, high variation in the values from the personal dosimeters worn by the astronauts were found. As a result of the Apollo missions, it was concluded that personal dosimeters worn by the astronauts should be combined with direct measurements taken at fixed locations inside the spacecraft to gather information about the radiation levels at different locations inside the space vehicle.
<p>
For the measurements, the radiation field was separated into few manageable components: into the sparsely and the densely ionizing radiation sections. Sparsely ionizing radiation consists of protons, electrons, gamma radiation and electromagnetic radiation, while densely ionizing radiation consists of neutrons, nuclear disintegrations and heavy ions.
<p>
APPROACH:<br>
Several different detector systems were used to measure the different radiation components. Each detector system absorbed and measured different radiation types or energy levels. The different materials used were layered in a sandwich-like manner in a stack. The radiation detectors used were cellulose nitrate, CR39, Lexan, nuclear emulsion and lithium fluoride thermoluminescence dosimeters.
<p>
Overall, 18 small (87 x 41 x 8 mm) and 3 large (96 x 64 x 64 mm) detector stacks were used during the D-2 mission. The smaller detector stacks were embedded in aluminized Kapton foil while the larger stacks were contained in hermetically sealed aluminum canisters.
<p>
One large detector stack was mounted on the Unique Support Structure (USS) in the Shuttle's payload bay. The other 18 small and 2 large detector stacks were mounted at different locations inside the D-2 Spacelab module and the tunnel, to represented a wide range of different cosmic radiation shieldings. After the space flight, the stacks were disassembled and the different detector materials "developed", similar to the development of a photo film.
<p>
RESULTS:<br>
The measured doses varied significantly. The measured doses for the biologically hazardous densely ionizing radiation ranged from 678 to 478 microGy for the 10-day D-2 mission. The highest measurements were obtained from the two stacks located in the Spacelab tunnel and one in the Spacelab end cone, followed by two stacks which were attached at an experiment rack near the end cone. The measurements taken by detector stacks attached to the front panel of other Spacelab racks did not differ significantly from each other and were significantly lower.
<p>
Although minor in their contribution to the absorbed dose (number of hits), the densely ionizing components contributed more than 50% to the dose equivalent. <end>
